### 6.6 Borehole Details

To prepare equipment adequately, basic details of the borehole will be required before arriving at the site. Certain systems with data recording facilities may also require the input of header information or supplementary comment. It is useful in any case to have as much data on the borehole as possible in order to aid on-site interpretation. A list of relevant data to be considered is as follows:

- a) Location and national grid reference;
- b) National water well number or other identifier;
- c) Total depth of the borehole;
- d) Standing water level and/or pumping water level;
- e) Date of construction;
- f) Drilling fluid, whether water, mud, air or foam;
- g) Lining or casing details including diameter, depth, plain or slotted, material (plastics, steel, fibreglass, etc), grouting details, construction of borehole top and datum levels;
- h) Uncased hole diameters and reductions;
- j) Borehole fluid;
- k) Zones of collapse or possible constriction;
- m) Presence of pump (dip tubes, flanged rising
main, electrical cables, etc);

- n) Likelihood of junk in borehole;
- p) Methane content of water;
- q) Presence of saline interfaces;
- r) Position of underground services or above ground cables;
- s) Pumping rates and possible influence of nearby abstraction of recharge boreholes; and
- t) Presence of contamination, whether from direct pollution into borehole, surface layers of oil or diesel, or leachate plumes.
